1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,721
Welcome to lectures twenty five and twenty six:

2
00:00:03,721 --> 00:00:07,046
feature engineering and feature selection.

3
00:00:07,046 --> 00:00:10,608
In this first video we build better features.

4
00:00:11,408 --> 00:00:13,721
Today's roadmap has two parts.

5
00:00:13,721 --> 00:00:14,415
Part one:

6
00:00:14,415 --> 00:00:23,822
build better features, with feature construction, interaction terms, polynomial features, and the curse of dimensionality.

7
00:00:23,822 --> 00:00:24,516
Part two:

8
00:00:24,516 --> 00:00:33,151
keep only useful features, with why we select, and filter, wrapper and embedded methods, then compare the three.

9
00:00:33,151 --> 00:00:37,778
Our running example is Online Shoppers Purchasing Intention:

10
00:00:37,778 --> 00:00:45,488
twelve thousand three hundred thirty website sessions, with the target Revenue, did the visitor buy?

11
00:00:46,288 --> 00:00:48,308
Here is why this matters.

12
00:00:48,308 --> 00:00:59,138
A logistic regression on the Online Shoppers data has a test R O C A U C of zero point eight five five with the ten original features.

13
00:00:59,138 --> 00:01:05,199
Add eight constructed features and it rises to zero point eight nine eight.

14
00:01:05,199 --> 00:01:10,613
Add three interaction terms and it reaches zero point nine oh four.

15
00:01:10,613 --> 00:01:17,321
That is a gain of zero point oh five without changing the model, only the features.

16
00:01:17,321 --> 00:01:20,392
Better data beats a fancier algorithm.

17
00:01:21,192 --> 00:01:24,808
Feature construction makes new columns from old.

18
00:01:24,808 --> 00:01:32,567
Combine existing columns using domain knowledge to make a column that states the pattern more directly.

19
00:01:32,567 --> 00:01:33,998
It is like cooking:

20
00:01:33,998 --> 00:01:39,422
the raw ingredients are already there, and the recipe makes them useful.

21
00:01:39,422 --> 00:01:56,975
For one session with three admin pages, zero informational and seventeen product pages over six hundred seconds, we construct total pages of twenty, average time per page of thirty seconds, and product share of zero point eight five.

22
00:01:56,975 --> 00:02:01,344
The model does not have to discover the pattern by itself.

23
00:02:02,144 --> 00:02:05,761
Five recipes for constructing features.

24
00:02:05,761 --> 00:02:06,318
Total:

25
00:02:06,318 --> 00:02:08,172
add related columns.

26
00:02:08,172 --> 00:02:08,729
Ratio:

27
00:02:08,729 --> 00:02:14,015
divide to remove a size effect, like duration over pages.

28
00:02:14,015 --> 00:02:14,479
Flag:

29
00:02:14,479 --> 00:02:20,229
turn a condition into one or zero, like page value above zero.

30
00:02:20,229 --> 00:02:20,600
Log:

31
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:30,246
shrink a long right tail; log of one plus page values takes skew from six point four to one point eight.

32
00:02:30,246 --> 00:02:31,080
And band:

33
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:37,016
cut a number into groups like short, medium, long and very long.

34
00:02:37,816 --> 00:02:40,866
One flag can split buyers from browsers.

35
00:02:40,866 --> 00:02:45,823
Has page value is one if the visitor saw a page that has a value.

36
00:02:45,823 --> 00:02:55,050
The purchase rate is fifty six point three percent when the flag is one, versus three point nine percent when it is zero:

37
00:02:55,050 --> 00:02:56,728
fourteen times higher.

38
00:02:56,728 --> 00:03:02,752
One line of code, and it becomes one of the two strongest features in the data.

39
00:03:03,552 --> 00:03:05,837
Bands show a step-like effect.

40
00:03:05,837 --> 00:03:17,263
Cutting total duration into four equal-size groups with qcut shows the purchase rate rising from four percent to twenty four percent, then flattening.

41
00:03:17,263 --> 00:03:25,032
Longer is better, but only up to a point, and a band lets even a simple model capture that flattening.

42
00:03:25,832 --> 00:03:27,510
Four good practices.

43
00:03:27,510 --> 00:03:29,860
Start from domain knowledge:

44
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:36,405
ask what an expert would look at; for shopping, effort, value, intent, season.

45
00:03:36,405 --> 00:03:38,168
Never use the target:

46
00:03:38,168 --> 00:03:46,475
a feature built from Revenue or future data is leakage, perfect in the lab and useless in real use.

47
00:03:46,475 --> 00:03:47,818
Guard divisions:

48
00:03:47,818 --> 00:03:55,538
ratios divide by zero for empty sessions, so replace zero with missing, then fill with zero.

49
00:03:55,538 --> 00:04:01,832
And test every new feature with the same model and split, before and after.

50
00:04:02,632 --> 00:04:06,740
An interaction means the effect of A depends on B.

51
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:11,504
Fertiliser helps a crop a lot, but only if there is water.

52
00:04:11,504 --> 00:04:16,680
A discount sells more, but much more during the festive season.

53
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:21,938
A linear model adds effects; it cannot multiply them on its own.

54
00:04:21,938 --> 00:04:26,209
So we give it the product A times B as a new column.

55
00:04:26,209 --> 00:04:31,960
If the coefficient b three is not zero, the slope of A changes with B.

56
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:36,021
Detect an interaction with a two-way table.

57
00:04:36,021 --> 00:04:46,637
Page value lifts new visitors from seven percent to eighty six percent, but returning visitors only from three percent to fifty one percent.

58
00:04:46,637 --> 00:04:48,988
Unequal jumps mean interaction.

59
00:04:48,988 --> 00:05:01,272
The new feature is page value times is returning, and A U C goes from zero point eight nine eight to zero point nine oh four after adding three interaction terms.

60
00:05:02,072 --> 00:05:12,711
Polynomial features add powers and all cross-products of chosen columns, letting a straight-line model fit curves and interactions.

61
00:05:12,711 --> 00:05:19,046
With inputs a and b at degree two you get a squared, a times b, and b squared.

62
00:05:19,046 --> 00:05:22,863
In Python, Polynomial Features with degree two.

63
00:05:22,863 --> 00:05:30,416
On real data, three inputs, page values, exit rates and product related, become nine columns.

64
00:05:31,216 --> 00:05:33,889
Columns explode as the degree rises.

65
00:05:33,889 --> 00:05:38,345
The number of columns is C of n plus d, choose d, minus one.

66
00:05:38,345 --> 00:05:55,648
Three inputs stay manageable, but ten inputs at degree three give two hundred eighty five columns, and our eighty five encoded features at degree three would give over one hundred nine thousand columns, for only twelve thousand rows.

67
00:05:56,448 --> 00:05:59,028
Use polynomial features safely.

68
00:05:59,028 --> 00:05:59,361
One:

69
00:05:59,361 --> 00:06:04,522
scale first, because x squared of a large number becomes huge.

70
00:06:04,522 --> 00:06:04,855
Two:

71
00:06:04,855 --> 00:06:14,011
keep the degree low; degree two is usually enough, and degree three and above mostly adds noise and over-fits.

72
00:06:14,011 --> 00:06:14,510
Three:

73
00:06:14,510 --> 00:06:24,665
pair with selection, expanding a few chosen columns and then using regularisation or feature selection to keep what helps.

74
00:06:24,665 --> 00:06:27,162
All three fit in one pipeline:

75
00:06:27,162 --> 00:06:33,072
polynomial features, standard scaler, and an L one logistic regression.

76
00:06:33,872 --> 00:06:35,954
The curse of dimensionality.

77
00:06:35,954 --> 00:06:39,599
Finding a friend on a hundred metre road is easy.

78
00:06:39,599 --> 00:06:42,053
On a hundred metre field, harder.

79
00:06:42,053 --> 00:06:44,954
In a hundred metre building, very hard.

80
00:06:44,954 --> 00:06:48,449
Every new feature is a new direction to search.

81
00:06:48,449 --> 00:07:06,968
With each feature cut into four bins there are four to the d cells, and the share of cells containing any of our twelve thousand sessions falls from seventy five percent with one feature to two percent at eight features and point two percent at ten.

82
00:07:07,768 --> 00:07:10,646
Three symptoms of too many features.

83
00:07:10,646 --> 00:07:11,605
Sparse data:

84
00:07:11,605 --> 00:07:19,759
with ten features only point two percent of the space has data, about five sessions per occupied cell.

85
00:07:19,759 --> 00:07:21,598
Distances lose meaning:

86
00:07:21,598 --> 00:07:30,071
in many dimensions the nearest and farthest points are almost equally far, so K N N and clustering suffer.

87
00:07:30,071 --> 00:07:31,430
And over-fitting:

88
00:07:31,430 --> 00:07:39,904
with many columns and few rows, the model memorises noise, giving a high train score and a low test score.

89
00:07:40,704 --> 00:07:42,340
Here is the evidence.

90
00:07:42,340 --> 00:07:46,468
Logistic regression trained on four hundred sessions:

91
00:07:46,468 --> 00:08:00,801
the train score climbs to zero point nine nine six as columns go from ten to two eighty five, but the test score falls from zero point eight seven eight to zero point seven four three.

92
00:08:00,801 --> 00:08:02,826
The model memorised noise.

93
00:08:02,826 --> 00:08:07,344
The extra two hundred seventy five columns hurt, not help.

94
00:08:08,144 --> 00:08:10,643
Five ways to beat the curse.

95
00:08:10,643 --> 00:08:11,536
More data:

96
00:08:11,536 --> 00:08:14,570
more rows per region of the space.

97
00:08:14,570 --> 00:08:16,177
Feature selection:

98
00:08:16,177 --> 00:08:18,408
keep only useful columns.

99
00:08:18,408 --> 00:08:19,747
Regularisation:

100
00:08:19,747 --> 00:08:24,031
penalise large coefficients with L one or L two.

101
00:08:24,031 --> 00:08:26,262
Dimensionality reduction:

102
00:08:26,262 --> 00:08:29,297
P C A and friends combine columns.

103
00:08:29,297 --> 00:08:31,171
And domain knowledge:

104
00:08:31,171 --> 00:08:34,027
build a few meaningful features.

105
00:08:34,027 --> 00:08:36,972
A rule of thumb for a classifier:

106
00:08:36,972 --> 00:08:42,149
aim for at least ten cases of the rarer class per feature.

107
00:08:42,149 --> 00:08:50,360
Pima diabetes has two hundred sixty eight positives, so about twenty seven features at most.

